<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/intermediate/impressionism-to-expressionism">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate art-history lesson. The foundational lesson <em>A Walk Through
    Western Art</em> supplies the map you need - the academic tradition these painters were
    trained in and rebelled against - and its four analysis questions (context, interaction,
    technique, interpretation) are the tools this lesson keeps sharpening. Everything else
    is built here.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — careless or calculated?" widget="judge" config={i2eCarelessJudge} />
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what the Salon controlled and how the 1874 independent exhibition rebuilt the art economy;</li>
    <li>connect materials and infrastructure - paint tubes, railways - and colour science to the Impressionist look;</li>
    <li>name the four Post-Impressionist exits and say what each kept from and refused of Impressionism;</li>
    <li>place Japonisme, Morisot and Cassatt inside the movement rather than at its footnotes;</li>
    <li>read distortion and expressive colour as evidence - Expressionism's answer to what painting is for.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    From Impressionism to Expressionism is <strong>painting's response to modernity and the
    camera</strong>: light, then structure, then feeling — each movement a different answer to
    "what is painting FOR now?" The half-century from 1863 to 1914 runs at revolutionary pace:
    a rejected painters' exhibition becomes a movement becomes a market; science (optics,
    colour theory) enters the studio as method; Japan rearranges Paris's compositions; and
    within fifty years the image detaches from matching the world entirely. It is also, on
    the syllabus's own terms, the best period to practise the four questions — context,
    interaction, technique, interpretation — because every one of them is changing at once.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="outdoors, structure, inner weather, the camera shadow" widget="equationStep" config={impCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> read the four stages as one escalation: painting leaves
    the studio, then re-imposes structure, then abandons the outside world for the inner one -
    with the camera's shadow behind all three moves. Each stage answers the same question,
    what is painting for, with a different and larger surrender of likeness.
  </p>

  <h2>Purpose and audience: the Salon system and its revolt</h2>
  <p>
    To understand the rebellion, know the institution: the Paris <strong>Salon</strong> was
    the sole gate to legitimacy, patronage and sales — annual, juried, academic taste
    (history painting first, polished surfaces, moral subjects). Painting left the studio
    because the system left no other door. 1863: the Salon rejects three-fifths of
    submissions; public uproar at the rejections forces Napoleon III to open the
    <strong>Salon des Refusés</strong>, where Manet's <em>Déjeuner sur l'herbe</em>
    scandalises the capital — and the art world learns that an alternative channel can
    exist. 1874: Monet, Renoir, Degas, Pissarro, Morisot, Sisley and friends exhibit
    independently at the photographer Nadar's studio; critic Louis Leroy mocks Monet's
    <em>Impression, soleil levant</em> (1872) — "Impressionism!" was an insult in
    <em>Le Charivari</em> — and the movement acquires its name from its enemies. The
    economics completed the model: dealer <strong>Durand-Ruel</strong> bankrolled the
    group through lean years and exported it (New York, 1886) — creating the modern
    circuit of dealer, group show, collector, critic that every later avant-garde
    would use. The lesson generalises: audience is manufactured by infrastructure,
    not found.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild the Salon revolt, from gate to market" widget="order" config={i2eSalonOrder} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the rebellion was institutional before it was stylistic - the
    Salon's gate, the Refusés, Nadar's studio, the dealer who bankrolled the alternative.
    What those painters actually did outdoors, with new tools and new science, is the next
    section's subject.
  </p>

  <h2>Materials and technique: the enabling revolutions</h2>
  <p>
    Impressionism's look is partly chemistry and infrastructure. <strong>The collapsible
    tin paint tube</strong> (John Goffe Rand, 1841) took pigment outdoors — Manet's quip
    that without tubes there would be no Impressionism is close to fair. The
    <strong>railway</strong> (the Paris suburban lines from the 1840s) put the Seine
    valley's leisure landscapes an hour from the studios — Argenteuil, Bougival — and
    painted the railway itself. The method: <strong>plein air</strong> painting fast,
    in front of the motif, catching light before it changes; <strong>broken
    brushwork</strong> — short, unblended strokes of unmixed colour — which mixed
    <em>optically</em> at viewing distance and made surfaces shimmer; and
    <strong>complementary colour science</strong> (Chevreul's simultaneous-contrast
    law was studio knowledge): shadows painted violet against orange light, not
    black — Monet's snow-shadow scandal of <em>The Magpie</em> (1869) made the
    physics visible. <strong>Worked experiment, the series</strong>: Monet painted the
    same Grainstacks (1890–91) and Rouen Cathedral façade (1892–94, 30-plus canvases)
    at different hours, tracking the motif's colour transformation — the subject stops
    being haystacks and becomes <em>light's effect on</em> haystacks; the series
    format is Impressionism's philosophy made method, and it is why the movement
    reads as an optical research program as much as a style.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each tool or law with what it changed" widget="match" config={i2eToolsMatch} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the Impressionists painted fast and loose because they
  were careless." The speed was the method, not the sloppiness: broken brushwork of unmixed
  colour was calculated to mix optically at viewing distance, and the violet shadows came
  from published colour science. Behind every shimmering surface sits a deliberate optical
  claim.</p>

  <h2>Interactions: Japonisme and the honest placement of Morisot and Cassatt</h2>
  <p>
    Two correctives belong here. First, <strong>Japonisme</strong>: ukiyo-e prints —
    Hokusai, Hiroshige — flooded Paris after Japan's 1867 Exposition Universelle
    debut; their flat colour fields, high horizons, cropped compositions and everyday
    subjects gave Degas his balconies and off-centre baths, Monet his garden and his
    print-collecting obsession, and Van Gogh his copies after Hiroshige. Western
    modernism's formal revolution is partly a cross-cultural import — decades before
    "art beyond the West" appeared in any curriculum. Second, <strong>Morisot and
    Cassatt are core, not footnote</strong>: Berthe Morisot exhibited in seven of the
    eight group shows and helped organise them, painting the domestic interiors the
    era's gender geography assigned her — and making them radical (loose handling on
    "proper" subjects); American Mary Cassatt joined in 1877 at Degas's invitation,
    her mothers-and-children paintings reversing the male gaze — women observing
    women with the era's modern vocabulary. <strong>Eva Gonzalès</strong> and
    <strong>Marie Bracquemond</strong> complete the four; a canon that says
    "Impressionism: Monet and Renoir" has simply not looked.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file the facts — the two footnote corrections" widget="sort-buckets" config={i2eCanonBuckets} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Western modernism invented its revolutions
  single-handedly." The composition-level evidence says otherwise: flat colour fields, high
  horizons and cropped framing arrived in Paris from Japanese prints after 1867 and were
  absorbed deliberately - Degas, Monet and Van Gogh studied and copied them. The formal
  revolution is partly a cross-cultural import.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pin the debut on the half-century line" widget="place" config={i2eJaponismePlace} />

  <h2>Post-Impressionism: four exits from the shimmer</h2>
  <p>
    The label (retrospective, coined by critic Roger Fry in 1910) hides four different
    dissatisfactions with pure sensation. <strong>Cézanne</strong> wanted Impressionism
    "solid and durable, like the art of the museums": constructional brushstrokes,
    colour-modulated planes, Mont Sainte-Victoire climbed thirty-odd times — his 1904
    advice ("treat nature by the cylinder, the sphere, the cone") is the recipe Picasso
    and Matisse credited ("the father of us all"); Cubism is his logical son.
    <strong>Seurat</strong> systematised: <em>A Sunday on La Grande Jatte</em>
    (1884–86) built from dots of pure colour by Chevreul-and-Rood rules — optical
    mixing as laboratory method (work the comparison: stirred complements dull;
    juxtaposed complements vibrate — the physics lesson's two mixing systems,
    deployed). <strong>Van Gogh</strong> redirected colour to feeling: the 1886 Paris
    encounter with Impressionism and Japanese prints lit the fuse; Arles (1888–89)
    produced the thick impasto, the directional strokes, and the letters that read as
    manifestos — The Night Café's red-and-green "terrible passions of humanity";
    <em>The Starry Night</em> (1889) as painted turbulence. <strong>Gauguin</strong>
    quit the metropolis (Brittany, then Tahiti 1891) for <em>synthetism</em> — flat
    zones of symbolic colour, black outlining (Cloisonnism) — whose "primitive"
    frame is itself now read critically (the colonial romance the paintings ran on
    is part of the evidence). Four exits; every road of the next century starts
    from one of them.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> four painters took the same shimmering inheritance in four
    directions - structure, system, feeling, symbol. The next section follows the feeling
    exit all the way: painting turns inward entirely.
  </p>

  <h2>Expressionism: the inner weather</h2>
  <p>
    By the 1890s the camera had likeness, the Impressionists had light — the remaining
    territory was <em>experience</em>. <strong>Edvard Munch</strong> drew the map:
    <em>The Scream</em> (1893) — the landscape's waves bent to the figure's anxiety,
    "I felt a great scream pass through nature" (the diary sentence behind the
    painting). <strong>Fauvism</strong> (Paris, 1905: Matisse, Derain, Vlaminck)
    detached colour from description entirely — <em>The Woman with the Hat</em>'s
    green stripe down the nose; the critic Vauxcelles's "wild beasts" (fauves)
    supplied the name. The German groups gave the movement its name and its politics:
    <strong>Die Brücke</strong> (Dresden, 1905: Kirchner, Heckel, Schmidt-Rottluff) —
    jagged woodcut-derived drawing, acid colour, urban alienation (<em>Berlin
    Street Scene</em>, 1913–14); <strong>Der Blaue Reiter</strong> (Munich, 1911:
    Kandinsky, Franz Marc) — colour as spiritual frequency (Kandinsky's
    <em>Concerning the Spiritual in Art</em>, 1911, and his first abstract
    compositions: painting finally leaving the object behind). Expressionism's
    interpretive frame: distortion as truthfulness — the perception lesson
    inverted, from how the world looks to how it feels to be alive in it —
    and the war would soon test that sincerity in ways the movement's history
    (Kirchner's breakdown; Nazi "Degenerate Art" seizure, 1937) made permanent.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="bet first — what was left to paint?" widget="predict" config={i2eTerritoryPredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Impressionist = pretty landscape." The movement's
  subjects were radical — suburban leisure, railway steam, boulevard crowds, laundry
  boats: the new Paris of Haussmann and department stores, painted with optical
  science and sold through a newly invented market. The prettiness was earned
  afterwards by calendar companies; the challenge was contemporary.</p>

  <h2>The wider fin de siècle, and the door to Cubism</h2>
  <p>
    Two expansions complete the map. First, the <strong>Symbolist</strong> current
    running beneath the movements: against Impressionism's objective eye, artists
    (Gauguin again, Odilon Redon, Gustave Moreau) proposed the image as idea and
    dream — colour and subject chosen for suggestion rather than description,
    literature supplying the theory (Mallarmé's circle) and music the model
    (Wagner, Debussy). Its decorative heir was <strong>Art Nouveau</strong>
    (1890s–1900s): the whiplash line of Horta and Guimard, the posters of
    Toulouse-Lautrec and Mucha, Klimt's gilded Vienna Secession — a style that
    dissolved the wall/ornament/fine-art boundaries the Salons policed, and
    whose design synthesis feeds directly into the 20th century's applied
    arts. Second, the terminus: <strong>Cubism at the door</strong>. Picasso's
    <em>Les Demoiselles d'Avignon</em> (1907) detonated the inherited picture —
    multiple viewpoints, compressed space, the African-mask borrowings the
    next lesson examines — and Braque joined him in the analytic Cubism of
    1909–12: Cézanne's planes facetted to their logic, the single viewpoint
    abandoned as a principle. Futurism (1909, Boccioni's manifestos) added
    time and speed to the same destructuring. The lineage is the period's
    summary: Impressionism freed colour and brushwork, Post-Impressionism
    freed structure and expression, and by 1912 the window itself — the
    Renaissance contract every previous lesson assumed — was on the floor,
    voluntarily.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="find the faulty line — the road to Cubism" widget="spot-error" config={i2eCubismSpot} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "Van Gogh painted that way because he was unstable." The
  myth compresses a working professional: he trained seriously, read constantly, and reasoned
  his colour choices in letters that read as manifestos - the Night Cafe's red-and-green is a
  stated theory, not a symptom. His breakdown belongs late in the story and explains some
  periods of it, not the method itself.</p>

  <h2>Three close readings</h2>
  <p>
    The period's arguments are best practised on three canvases.
    <strong>Monet, <em>Impression, soleil levant</em> (1872)</strong>: the orange
    sun and its reflection are near-identical in value — remove the colour
    and the sun nearly vanishes, a deliberate demonstration (Chevreul's
    simultaneous contrast) that <em>hue</em> can do the work of luminosity;
    the harbour's silhouettes dissolve into worked atmosphere. Leroy's
    mockery was a response to exactly this unfinished-seeming surface.
    <strong>Seurat, <em>A Sunday on La Grande Jatte</em> (1884–86)</strong>:
    despite the pointillist reputation, close looking shows most dots
    applied over restated underpainting, with complementary dots
    woven at edges — the science is at the boundaries, where
    contrast lives; and the frieze-like stillness (figures
    profiled, hieratic) shows the Classicist under the
    optical researcher. <strong>Van Gogh, <em>The Starry
    Night</em> (1889)</strong>: the sky's strokes are
    directional — swirling around the stars, streaming past
    the cypress like fire — paint handling carrying
    turbulence the drawing alone would not; the village
    below sits in calm verticals, and the composition's
    logic (flame left, wave right, church spire
    steadying the middle) shows a constructed,
    not hallucinated, picture. Three works,
    three theses: colour as light, optics as
    method, brushwork as feeling — the
    period in one paragraph each.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="drag the divider — Monet's sun, with and without hue" widget="compare" config={i2eSunCompare} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three canvases carry three theses - colour as light, optics as
    method, brushwork as feeling. Test yourself against them before the deeper questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Monet paints the same grainstack at seven in the morning and again at five in the afternoon. What is the real subject of each canvas?</li>
    <li>Why do Seurat's juxtaposed dots of blue and orange look livelier than the same two pigments stirred together?</li>
    <li>By 1890 the camera could reproduce a face exactly. Name one thing painting could still do that photography could not.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Light's effect on the stack: the series format turns the
    motif into an optical variable, so the subject is time and colour, not hay - Impressionism's
    philosophy made method. (2) Stirred complements produce a dull neutral (physical,
    subtractive mixing); juxtaposed dots mix in the eye at viewing distance and keep their
    vibrancy - optical mixing systematised from colour science. (3) Duration, sensation,
    subjectivity: fleeting light, expressive colour, inner states - the whole redirection
    from Impressionism to Expressionism is painting moving onto ground the camera cannot
    reach.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Impressionism means painting leaves the studio: fast, outdoors, optical, contemporary.</li>
    <li>The Salon was the gate; the 1874 show and Durand-Ruel built the alternative circuit every later avant-garde reused.</li>
    <li>Broken brushwork and complementary colour science were method, not mannerism.</li>
    <li>Post-Impressionism is four dissatisfactions: Cezanne structure, Seurat system, Van Gogh feeling, Gauguin symbol.</li>
    <li>Morisot and Cassatt are core; Japonisme is a structural import, not a footnote.</li>
    <li>Expressionism turns painting inward: distortion as truthfulness, colour as psychology.</li>
    <li>Photography took likeness; painting answered by doing what the camera cannot.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson <em>Art Beyond the West</em> is this period's own next chapter - the
    African-mask borrowings and the Japonisme asymmetry both point there - while <em>Renaissance
    &amp; Baroque</em> shows what the rebels were rebelling against. The advanced lesson
    <em>Modern &amp; Contemporary Art</em> picks up exactly where this one ends: Cubism on the
    floor and abstraction waiting in Kandinsky's studio.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Interpretations: two reviews that made two histories.</strong> Leroy's 1874
    mockery accidentally wrote the movement's name — a reminder that criticism
    co-authors art history (read it: the review is genuinely funny and genuinely
    wrong in the ways period taste was wrong). At the other pole, the romantic
    Van Gogh myth (mad genius, sells one painting) compresses a working professional:
    he trained seriously, read constantly, worked from intensive correspondence —
    the Theo letters are among art history's great documents — and his posthumous
    fame is partly his sister-in-law Jo van Gogh-Bonger's promotional labour. Both
    cases teach the syllabus's interpretations lens: trace claims to sources, and
    ask who profits from the story.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The science angle, maturely held.</strong> The Impressionists absorbed
    contemporary optics (Chevreul 1839, Rood's <em>Modern Chromatics</em> — the
    book Cézanne-era studios argued over) but were painters, not physicists: the
    correspondence is inspiration and vocabulary, not literal application — and
    modern colour science (opponent channels) shows the paintings work for reasons
    partly different from the ones claimed. A good essay knows both layers: what
    they said they were doing, and what the eye is actually doing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How much did photography shape composition
    directly (cropped, high-angle, candid frames appear in painting before any
    painter admits looking — Degas's ambiguities are the test case)? Was the
    avant-garde's market independence a liberation or a new dependence (dealers
    replaced juries; taste moved from Salon to collection)? And the global ledger:
    Japonisme sans reciprocity — Europe took prints; empire took markets — is the
    period's asymmetry now being written into its history, with Japonisme as the
    case study the next lesson's "beyond the West" picks up. The half-century ends
    with abstraction waiting in Kandinsky's studio and Cubism waiting in Cézanne's
    apples: modern art's whole future is visible from here, which is why examiners
    love the period and students should too.
  </p>
